        'synopsis' => 'A superstitious schoolmaster courts the richest young woman of a drowsy Hudson valley town until a midnight rival prank sends him fleeing forever.',
        'text' => "On the eastern shore of the Hudson, at a wide part of the river, there stood a little market town whose busy housewives named it Tarry Town, because their husbands were apt to tarry long at the village inn on market days. Two miles back from the river lay a small valley shut in by treacherous green hills, and this quiet hollow was known to everyone for miles around as Sleepy Hollow. Something in the very air of the place seemed to weigh upon the eyelids and slow the footsteps, so that the people who lived there dreamed waking dreams, heard strange voices in the passing wind, and believed in ghosts as firmly as they believed in spring planting.\n\nThe chief spirit that haunted this enchanted region was the Headless Horseman. Old country folk said he was the ghost of a Hessian trooper, a hired soldier of the late war, whose head had been carried away by a cannon ball in some nameless battle. They declared that he rode out every night to the field where he lost it, galloping through the dark in search of that missing head, and that he must be back in his churchyard grave before daybreak.\n\nInto this dreaming valley there came, many years ago, a schoolmaster from Connecticut named Ichabod Crane. He was tall and exceedingly thin, with narrow shoulders, long arms that swung far out of his sleeves, hands that dangled a mile below his coat cuffs, and feet that might have served for shovels. His head was small at the top and broad at the bottom, his ears were large like greenhouse doors, his mouth was enormous, and when he stretched it in a smile it ran from ear to ear. On a windy day he looked for all the world like a famished scarecrow blown loose from its cornfield.\n\nHe kept his school in a low log house at the foot of a woody hill, and ruled his scholars with a birch rod, though he was careful to spare the pupils who had pretty sisters or dainty mothers. In the long winter evenings he sat by the fire of some old Dutch farmhouse, eating apples with the grandmothers and listening while they told tales of haunted fields and haunted bridges and haunted houses, and most of all of the Headless Horseman. These stories delighted him strangely, and afterward, walking homeward in the dark, he believed every one of them with all his heart. Every gust rattling a fence rail became a hoofbeat, and the far hoot of an owl a dying groan. To keep his courage up he sang loud psalm tunes all down the lane, and the melody floated mournfully through the night like the voice of a frightened boy.\n\nBut Ichabod's soft heart, though it was a very small one, had two tender places in it, and these were his appetite and his love. The object of his love was Katrina Van Tassel, the only child of a well-to-do farmer, a blooming young woman as plump as a partridge, ripe and rosy cheeked, and famous through the whole countryside for her beauty. And truly Ichabod's mouth watered whenever he passed her father's gate, for his fancy saw beyond the girl herself the wonderful things her inheritance would one day set before him. He imagined fat pigs wandering the orchard with apples in their mouths, geese sailing comfortably in the pond, turkeys strutting with swollen cheeks, rows of smoking pies, and honey dripping from the hollow oaks. His heart yearned after the vegetables, and his whole soul was filled with dreams of butter and eggs.\n\nBetween this dreaming schoolmaster and his hopes there stood one tremendous obstacle, and that obstacle was Brom Van Brunt, known to every soul in the county as Brom Bones. He was broad shouldered and double jointed, with short curly black hair and a bearish stride, and he was famed for his knowledge of horses and his boldness in every sport. He had three or four boon companions who rode with him from farm to farm on winter nights, and when the thunder of their galloping hoofs shook the windows, the dogs barked, the children crept closer to their mothers, and the old wives knew well enough that it was only Brom Bones and his gang.\n\nBrom had also cast his eye upon Katrina, and he did not love a rival. Yet Ichabod was too crafty to be drawn into a fair fight, so Brom contented himself with mischief. The schoolhouse door was found blocked with brush one Monday morning. A dog was taught to whine whenever Ichabod lifted his voice in psalmody. Even the chimney refused to draw, filling the schoolroom with smoke, and the pedagogue suspected sorcery when it was only Brom's boys on the roof.\n\nOne fine autumn day there arrived an invitation to a great quilting frolic at the Van Tassel farm. Ichabod could not ride his own horse, for the schoolmaster's steed was a sorry animal at best, so he borrowed a broken-down plow horse called Gunpowder from the farmer Hans Van Ripper. Gunpowder was a gaunt and mischievous creature with an evil eye and a habit of sudden sideways jerks, and Ichabod, perched on his high saddle, jogged off to the merrymaking looking like a scarecrow escaped into the dusk.\n\nNever had the schoolmaster seen such a feast. There were plump pigeons and succulent beef, a roast pig that stood with an apple in its mouth, ducks and geese swimming in rivers of gravy, apple pies and peach pies and pumpkin pies, doughnuts and sweet cakes, and bowls of cream that the youngest girls skimmed as fast as they could fill them. Ichabod did ample justice to every dish, and afterward he danced, and no man in the country could dance like Ichabod Crane. His pride swelled as he stamped and spun with Katrina, and he felt sure his fortune was made.\n\nLater the company gathered around the fire, and the old women told their best ghost stories, and the tales grew darker with every candle. Brom Bones told how he had met the Headless Horseman one midnight and challenged him to race for a bowl of punch, and how the specter kept beside him to the very church bridge and then vanished in a flash of fire. An old gentleman added that he had seen the Horseman that very morning, hurrying through the hollow toward Sleepy Hollow church.\n\nIchabod stayed until the last guest had gone, and he went home with a heavy heart. For the truth was that Katrina, when he had found her alone that evening and poured out his feelings, had received them coldly and dismissed him. The night was dark, the wind moaned in the bare trees, and every shadow of his road came alive with the stories of the evening. As he drew near the church where the road crossed the bridge, he thought he saw something huge and black rising out of the gloom beside the stream.\n\nIt was a horse of great size and blackness, and upon its back sat a rider, tall and vast in the silver moonlight. Ichabod's heart died within him, for the rider wore no head upon his shoulders, and the head itself, dark and terrible, was bound at the saddle bow. The pedagogue dug his heels into Gunpowder and fled. Neck and neck they thundered up the hill, the strange rider keeping pace with terrible ease, until they came to the bridge. There the goblin rose in his stirrups and hurled his head straight at the flying schoolmaster. It struck Ichabod full in the face, knocked him from his saddle, and the world went dark around him.\n\nThe next morning Gunpowder was found cropping grass at his master's gate, his saddle galled and his bridle torn. Searchers discovered Ichabod's hat lying by the road near the church bridge, and close beside it a shattered pumpkin.\n\nThe schoolmaster was never seen in Sleepy Hollow again. Some said the Galloping Hessian had claimed him at last. But an old farmer, who had been down to New York, brought word that a man answering Ichabod's description had been seen there studying law, and that he had since been made a justice and written pieces for the newspapers. He had left the hollow, said the farmer, because Katrina had mocked his suit and he dared not stay in a place so full of ghosts.\n\nBrom Bones married Katrina before the season was out, and it was noticed that he laughed loudly whenever anyone mentioned the pumpkin. The old Dutch wives, however, know better, and to this day they will tell you that the Headless Horseman rides the hollow still, and that schoolmasters who linger late should hurry past the bridge.",
