        'synopsis' => 'A young Union sentry wakes on a cliff at his post and finds a Confederate scout watching his hidden army, and the scout is his own father.',
        'text' => "One sunny afternoon far out in western Virginia, a body of the Union army lay hidden in the forest along a road that wound down toward a stream. Five regiments waited there among the pines, silent and unseen, for the road below was the one the enemy would use if he meant to cross the mountains. High above them, on the very edge of a gigantic cliff, slept one soldier of the Federal cavalry.\n\nHe was Carter Druse, and he had chosen this post himself. To fall asleep at a sentry post in the face of an active enemy was a crime that could be punished by nothing less than death, and every man in the army knew it. Yet exhaustion had taken him the moment he sat down against the great rock, for he had been awake through two nights of hard marching.\n\nCarter Druse was the son of wealthy parents who lived not many miles from this very field, in the hill country where he had been born. He was calm and kindly, a boy fond of horses and of books, and when the war broke out he thought for a while that it was a foolish business and that his duty lay at home. But one morning he told his father quietly that a Union regiment had been raised in the Carolina mountains, and that he meant to go to it. The mother was failing fast, and her heart could scarcely bear the thought of parting. The old gentleman looked at his son for a long moment without speaking. Then he lifted his head and said, \"Well, go, sir, and whatever may occur, do what you conceive to be your duty.\" And Carter bowed low and left the house without another word.\n\nThe young man had kept faithfully awake until now, though his post was terribly lonely. From the cliff top he commanded the whole valley below, and no movement of soldiers or of wagons could pass along the winding road without his seeing it. He lay with his carbine across his knees, watching the sunlight crawl down the face of the rock, and at last his eyes closed in spite of everything.\n\nWhen he raised his head again, his hand went quickly to his brow and then to his carbine, and he lay perfectly still, hardly daring to breathe. Across the valley, on a ledge of rock that jutted from the opposite cliff, stood a horseman in the full light of the western sky. Horse and rider were so still, so grandly outlined against the blue heavens, that they seemed carved in bronze. The bay horse stood without motion, and the rider sat erect and soldierly, looking down into the hidden valley as calmly as a general reviewing a map.\n\nAn artist would have loved the picture, for it had the noble quality of some heroic statue. But Carter Druse did not think of art. His first feeling was one of strange admiration, and his second was one of cold horror, for he saw what the statue meant. The horseman had found the road. He had discovered the sleeping army, and he sat there taking his ease only because he believed no eye could reach him.\n\nThen the blood left Carter's face, and his hand shook so that he had to press it flat upon the stone. He knew that horse. He knew the proud arch of its neck and the white star upon its forehead, for it had grazed in his own father's pasture all the summer before the war. And the man who sat so straight in the saddle wore the uniform of a Confederate scout, and that man was his father.\n\nThe moments passed like hours. Below in the forest lay five regiments, thousands of lives that would be lost if the scout rode home with what he knew. Above, against the sky, sat the one man on earth whose life the young soldier had been bred to guard. Duty had conquered already, but his hand trembled yet, and he grew pale and faint with the effort of steadying it. He looked once more at the noble figure against the sky. Then he laid his carbine carefully to his cheek, took aim, and fired.\n\nA Union officer riding slowly up the road below heard the crack of the shot and started. At that instant something fell over the edge of the great cliff, a horse and its rider plunging together through the air, whirling downward past ledge after ledge until they were swallowed by the depths of the forest. The officer sat as though turned to stone. Then he turned his horse and rode away slowly, with his head sunk upon his breast, and he said nothing to any man of what he had seen.\n\nBefore long a sergeant crept cautiously to the post on the cliff. He found Carter Druse lying white and unconscious, his carbine still gripped in his hands, his face like the face of a dead man. The sergeant shook him gently by the shoulder.\n\n\"Did you fire?\" the sergeant asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"At what?\"\n\n\"A horse,\" said Carter faintly. \"It was standing on yonder rock.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at him strangely. \"Was anyone on the horse?\"\n\nThe young man's lips moved, and his voice sank almost to a whisper. \"Yes.\"\n\n\"Well? Who was it?\"\n\n\"My father.\"\n\nThe sergeant rose to his feet and walked away. \"Good God!\" he said.",
