        'synopsis' => 'A man abandoned with a sprained ankle in the frozen north crawls for weeks through starvation and wolves, kept alive by nothing but the love of life.',
        'text' => "They limped painfully down the bank of a white stream in the frozen north, two men heavily burdened with blanket packs, rifles slung on their shoulders. The one in front staggered among the rough stones without ever looking back. The one behind had slipped that morning and turned his ankle badly.\n\n\"I say, Bill, I've sprained my ankle,\" he called.\n\nBill staggered on through the milky water. He did not turn his head. Behind him came the other cry, loud and pleading, the cry of a strong man in trouble, calling \"Bill!\" over and over into the empty land. But Bill's head never turned once, and his stumbling figure grew smaller down the valley until it was gone.\n\nThen the man was alone, and there was no fear in him at first, only a strange emptiness. He sat down in the moss and took stock of what he had. His rifle was empty, every cartridge long since fired away. There was a blanket, a few strips of rawhide, and a heavy sack of gold dust he had panned out of the streams in better days. The gold weighed like iron, yet he could not bring himself to throw it away. He bound his ankle tight, rose, and hobbled south, telling himself over and over that Bill would wait for him at their cache under the overturned canoe, and would surely not desert him.\n\nThe days passed in a gray blur of fog and drizzle. He ate muskeg berries that tasted bitter and left him emptier than before. He crouched at pools trying to catch minnows with his bare hands, and the little fish always flashed away at the last instant. Once he caught a young ptarmigan chick with a thrown stone and ate it raw, and dreamed all night afterward of rich feasts he had eaten in another life. He chewed strips of soaked rawhide from his pack. Rain fell, nights froze, and still he dragged himself southward, mile after mile, counting nothing but his hunger.\n\nA wolf began to follow him. It was old and sick, thin as a rail, and it kept always fifty paces behind or beside him, waiting with the patience of its kind. He shouted at it and it stood still, coughing. By degrees he understood that they were both doing the same thing, trailing each other across the waste, each hoping the other would fall first.\n\nOne afternoon he came upon scattered bones lying where the wolves had fed, and beside them a blanket pack exactly like his own, and a heavy sack of gold. He knew them well. They were Bill's. The man looked at the bones a long time, and then he laughed, a shrill and terrible laugh that frightened even himself. Bill had deserted him, and this was Bill's reward. But he did not touch Bill's gold, and though his own hunger was madness, he turned from those bones and went on.\n\nAfter that he gave up walking. His feet no longer served, so he crawled, an inch at a time, dragging his pack after him until even the gold was flung away piece by piece. The sick wolf crawled too, wheezing at his heels, closer now with every day, patient beyond all believing. Sometimes the man slept and woke to find the hot breath on his hand.\n\nThen one morning he saw something that made him close his eyes and open them again. Down where the land ended there lay a shining sea, and riding at anchor in that sea was a ship. He knew very well that no ships belonged in the heart of this dead land, and he told himself it was a dream. But the dream would not go away. It was real. A shining sea, the Arctic Ocean, and a whaler strayed far from her course, and men aboard her.\n\nFor five days he crawled toward that ship, and the sick wolf coughed along behind, closing the distance day by day. At last neither of them could crawl anymore, and they lay within reach of each other, waiting. The patience of the wolf was terrible, and the patience of the man was no less terrible. Half a day they lay so. Then the man dozed, and felt a tongue drag gently along his hand, and the teeth press softly down upon it. His fingers closed on the wolf's jaw, weakly at first, then with a grip. His other hand crept across. And when it was done, he pressed his face against the animal's throat and forced himself to swallow the warm trickle that came, because his will commanded what his body refused.\n\nThere were men of a scientific expedition aboard the whale ship Bedford, and from her deck they saw a strange object moving down the shore toward the water. Being scientific men, they climbed into the whale boat and rowed ashore to study it. What they found was alive, but it could hardly be called a man. It wriggled along the ground like some great worm, blind and unconscious, covering perhaps twenty feet in an hour.\n\nThree weeks later that man lay in a bunk on the Bedford and wept as he told who he was and what he had suffered. He babbled of his mother, and of sunny Southern California, and of a home among orange groves and flowers. Soon he sat at table with the officers and scientists, watching every mouthful that went into other men's mouths with such longing that the sight hurt them. He questioned the cook and the captain about the food stores a dozen times a day, and could never quite believe their answers.\n\nThen the sailors noticed something funny. The man was growing fat. The scientists set limits to his meals and studied him gravely, but his girth increased. The sailors grinned, for they knew. They saw how he begged scraps with open palm, and they slipped him pieces of hard sea biscuit which he hid like a miser's treasure. At last the scientists examined his bunk in private. The mattress was stuffed with hardtack, the corners were lined with hardtack, and every crack and cranny was packed with it. Yet he was sane enough. He was simply taking precautions against another famine.\n\nAnd he recovered from all of it before the anchor of the Bedford rumbled down into San Francisco Bay.",
