        'synopsis' => 'From his death row cell a condemned man confesses how alcohol, a wronged black cat, and his own perverse heart carried him from kindness to the gallows wall.',
        'text' => "Tomorrow I die, and I would unburden my soul while I still may. What I am about to set down is no dream. Terror and destruction came of these things, yet through it all ran something so ordinary that a man might smile at it, if he did not shudder. From my infancy I was noted for the gentleness of my disposition. My companions joked about my softness of heart, and my parents seemed proud of a boy who could not pass a suffering creature by. I married early, and my wife saw in my love of animals a promise of tenderness. We had birds, gold fish, a fine dog, rabbits, a small monkey, and a cat.\n\nThe cat was the largest of our household and the wisest, jet black without a single white hair, and remarkably intelligent. When I spoke of him, people smiled at the fancy, but Pluto, as I named him for the god of the underworld, was truly my favorite pet and playmate, and he followed me about the house and yard for years.\n\nThen came the change. Strange to tell, the disease of drink grew upon me, and with it a temper that turned on everything I loved. I neglected the pets, and worse than neglected them. Only Pluto still felt the old kindness in me. But one night, coming home inflamed with liquor, I fancied the cat avoided my touch. A fury seized me. I caught the poor animal, and with my penknife I cut one of its eyes from the socket. I blush, I burn, I shudder while I write it.\n\nWhen reason returned with morning, the thing I had done lay black upon my soul. Pluto recovered from the wound, but the socket presented a frightful appearance, and he fled my approach with painful slowness, as if from habit rather than hatred. At first I grieved. Then, step by step, something darker took their place, and that spirit was perverseness, the impulse of the human heart to do wrong for no reason except that it knows the wrong is wrong. Philosophy has never explained this spirit, yet every man has felt it tugging at him when he stood on the very edge of doing harm.\n\nOne morning, in cold blood, I slipped a noose about Pluto's neck and hanged him from the branch of a tree. I hung the thing because I knew it had loved me, because it had given me not one reason for the act, and because my soul thirsted for its own ruin. With tears streaming down my face and the bitterest remorse in my heart, I did the deed. On the night of that same day a cry of fire woke us from sleep. The whole house burned, and only one wall remained standing. Crowds gathered before it, and when the smoke thinned, a shout of horror rose from all who looked. Plastered fresh upon the standing wall, in white relief against the blackness, was the figure of a gigantic cat. There was the beast, drawn accurately as by an artist's hand, and about its neck hung the image of the rope.\n\nFor months I could not explain that image, though I tried, telling myself that some neighbor had thrown the dead body into my window, and that fire, plaster, and grief had worked together on the wall. Then one night in a low tavern I found a black cat of enormous size sitting among the barrels. It was fully as large as Pluto and closely resembled him in every respect but one. Pluto had not a single white hair. This creature had a large, though indefinite, splotch of white covering its breast. I stroked it, it followed me home, and my wife, whose heart was gentle toward every living thing, was glad enough to keep it.\n\nSoon I came to dislike the beast. It showed, like Pluto, the loss of one eye, and this began to disgust and terrify me. Worse, the white patch upon its breast grew slowly defined, until at last I could bear the shape no longer. It had become unmistakably the figure of the GALLOWS. And the cat followed me everywhere with its fondness, always underfoot, always watching, and I fled its presence as I would have fled the plague, too much ashamed to strike it and too fearful to destroy it, remembering the old affection and the new dread together.\n\nOne day my wife went with me into the cellar on some household errand. The cat followed down the steep steps, and in the dark it threw itself against my legs so nearly as to upset me. Madness came in a breath. I lifted the axe, forgetting even my hate of the beast in the fury of the moment, and swung it full at the creature's head. My wife stayed my arm with her hand. And the axe descended where she stood instead. She fell without a cry, and there was silence in the cellar.\n\nI have said that I am monstrous, and here is the proof of what a man may do and then plan calmly. The walls of the cellar were thick, and along one side ran an old chimney passage now filled with brick. There was room enough for a body behind a false facing of mortar and stone. I worked through the night, laid the body upright against the inner wall, built my facing over it, and finished so smoothly that no eye could have found the seam. I cleaned the axe and put away my tools, and I slept the sleep of the secure.\n\nOn the fourth day the police came again and searched the house thoroughly from attic to cellar. In the cellar they found nothing, and they turned to go up the stairs. Then my confidence became exultation. Seeking to make sure of my triumph, I rapped loudly with my cane upon the very bricks behind which my wife lay.\n\nAt the sound there came a voice from within the tomb. A cry at first, half horror and half triumph, such as a child might raise in agony, then swiftly swelling into one long, loud, continuous scream, a wailing howl unlike any sound of this earth. The officers stood frozen. I shook from head to foot as though with the falling fever. Why did I not die? With heavy blows they tore down the wall before my eyes. In the niche stood the corpse, black with rot, and clotted hair streamed about it. Upon its head sat the hideous beast, its red mouth wide open, its solitary eye of fire blazing up into mine.\n\nI had walled the monster up within the tomb.",
