        'synopsis' => 'Paris police tear apart a minister\'s hotel for three months and find nothing, until a poet detective suggests the stolen letter was never hidden at all.',
        'text' => "One evening in an autumn of the old Paris days, the Prefect of the city police called at the little library where my friend C. Auguste Dupin and I sat smoking after dark. The Prefect was a man who never came without trouble, and this time his trouble was great.\n\n\"You may remember a certain royal lady,\" he began, \"and how much depends upon her keeping her private papers safe. There is a document in the hands of her enemy which would give him terrible power over her, and it has been taken from her chamber.\"\n\n\"Taken,\" said Dupin quietly, \"by the Minister himself. That bold man entered while she sat alone, and before her eyes he lifted a letter from the table and put another in its place. She dared not cry out.\"\n\n\"She dared not,\" agreed the Prefect. \"The Minister stands too near the King. If she accused him, he could deny everything, and later produce the letter at the moment that would destroy her. She cannot seize it openly, and so she has turned to us.\"\n\n\"And what have you done?\"\n\n\"Everything.\" The Prefect counted on his fingers. \"I have searched his hotel for three months with agents I can trust. We probed every cushion with long needles. We took the tops off every table and examined them under a microscope. We opened every book by its binding, fearing a hollow cover. We lifted the carpets, sounded the cellar bricks one by one, pricked through the wallpaper, and even drained the lamp oil to be sure no folded paper lay inside it. The reward now stands at fifty thousand francs. And there is nothing, Monsieur Dupin. Nothing at all.\"\n\nDupin smiled a little behind his pipe. \"Perhaps the mystery is a little too plain,\" he said. \"Go back and search once more. Only look, this time, where it seems foolish to look.\"\n\nThe Prefect laughed bitterly, thanked us without hope, and left.\n\nA month passed before he returned, worn out and angry. The reward had been doubled and the search repeated from cellar to garret, still without success. Asked whether he remembered the exact appearance of the letter, he gave the description readily. It was a small delicate note, unaddressed, closed with a black seal.\n\nDupin drew open a checkbook and invited the Prefect to fill in a check for fifty thousand francs. When that was done, he went to a drawer, unlocked it, took out a letter, and laid the paper on the table.\n\nThe Prefect stared as though struck dumb. Then he seized the document, read it twice through, signed the check with a trembling hand, and rushed out of the room without finding a single word to say.\n\nWhen he had gone, Dupin explained himself to me.\n\n\"I knew a schoolboy game of guessing,\" he said. \"One boy holds counters behind his back and asks how many he holds, and the clever player watches his opponent's face instead of his hands, for the face always answers the question. It is the same with all hiding. The ordinary mind hides by burying, and so the searcher digs only where something might be buried. But the Minister is no ordinary mind. He is a mathematician and a poet as well, and your police agents are neither. They assume that the cleverest hiding is the deepest concealment. A man like that assumes they will assume exactly that.\"\n\n\"So he hid the letter in the light,\" I said slowly.\n\n\"In full daylight,\" said Dupin. \"I paid him a visit some weeks ago, wearing green spectacles against his curiosity, and studied the room as we talked. On a card rack hanging beneath the mantelpiece, among papers anyone might see, there hung one crumpled note with a great black seal bearing his cipher, addressed in a woman's handwriting to himself. It was the letter, altered in outward show and thrust carelessly into the uppermost division of the rack. Her own agent had stood beside that rack and looked straight past it. And the Minister left it there because he counted on the blindness of every man who searches for what is hidden.\"\n\n\"But how did you take it?\" I asked.\n\n\"The next day I called again, carrying the facsimile copy I had made at home. While we spoke, a man in the street below fired a musket among a crowd, and the Minister sprang to the window. In that instant I exchanged the two papers, dropped the true letter into my pocket, and left my copy in its place on the rack.\"\n\n\"And the copy? Was it harmless?\"\n\n\"It carries a small message, written in a pointed place, for the Minister's eye alone,\" said Dupin. \"It tells him whose hand has returned his blow. So deadly a design, if it is not worthy of Atreus, is certainly worthy of Thyestes.\"",
