        'synopsis' => 'Trapped on a cliff ledge by an enemy patrol with no water, a British officer improvises a parachute from tent canvas and accidentally becomes a mountain legend.',
        'text' => "The Ethnologist had come four hundred miles out of his way to collect the old stories of the hill people before the missionaries and the army changed them forever, and instead he had collected something he did not expect. Now he sat across the camp table from a sandy-haired infantry lieutenant and let him have his feelings plainly.\n\n\"Invulnerable, they say you are,\" he complained. \"You can jump over elephants. You can fly. One old gentleman described your wings to me, black plumage, not quite as long as a mule, and swore he saw you hovering over the crests by moonlight. These are children of the mountains, man. How could you trick them so?\"\n\nThe lieutenant laughed cheerfully. \"I am sorry,\" he said, \"but truly it was forced upon me. Let me explain the business, since you seem aggrieved.\"\n\nHe settled back in his folding chair and began. It was during one of the border expeditions, and his colonel believed the villages of that gorge were friendly. So the lieutenant was sent up fourteen miles of river valley to judge the temper of the people, with three soldiers from Derbyshire, half a dozen Sepoys, two mules, and his commanding officer's blessing.\n\n\"The villagers answered my visit with a lump of filed brass idol fired down the slope,\" said the lieutenant. \"It missed my shoulder by an inch and killed the mule that carried our provisions. And then we saw matchlock men moving along the ridge between us and the village. I gave the word to turn about, not too close together, and off we went down the valley at a smart trot.\"\n\nThe retreat became ugly. Halfway down, scouts appeared on the hills ahead of them, cutting them off from the narrow part of the river.\n\n\"They could have shot every one of us at fifty yards in the gorge,\" said the lieutenant. \"So we climbed instead, following a shelf of road along the hillside. A Sepoy took a bullet in the leg and sat down quietly without a word. Then we rounded a corner of rock and found the shelf simply ended. Above, the cliff hung over us so no one could roll stones down. Below was a precipice of three hundred feet. The only way on was the way we had come, and one man behind those rocks was as good as a regiment.\"\n\nThey settled onto the ledge to wait for relief, and there he told how the trouble showed itself plainly. Their only store of food and water stood in one live mule, and the river that mocked them lay far below, going giggle around a rock in the sunshine.\n\n\"That first day we wrung what dew we could out of our clothes and the tent,\" he went on. \"One of the Derbyshire men said something none of us heard and walked off round the bend after shooting at shadows. By morning the wounded Sepoy was out of his head and jumped or fell, I never learned which. We shot the mule for meat, and the poor beast leapt the edge in its struggles and was gone too. That left eight of us.\"\n\n\"And the second day?\"\n\n\"Nobody spoke. Our lips were black and our throats afire. I lay looking over the edge at the water rippling down there, and I will confess it seemed a pleasant thing to go rushing through the air toward the river and have done with thirst forever. Remembering that I was the officer, and owed the men a good example, I stopped myself. And thinking of the example gave me the idea.\"\n\nHe looked at the Ethnologist with great satisfaction.\n\n\"I cut a big circle of canvas from the tent and plugged the hole in its center, and tied eight ropes round its rim to meet in the middle. The men watched me build it as if I had gone mad already. When night came, the two British soldiers held the thing above my head while I ran the length of the ledge. At the edge I stopped dead, I confess it. Then I was ashamed in front of privates, walked back, and jumped the second time, with a kind of sob, clean into the dark, with the white sail bellying out over me.\"\n\n\"Did it hold?\"\n\n\"It heeled once, and then I saw the face of the rock streaming upward past me while I stayed still, which is the correct arrangement. Below, the river came up to meet me, and with it three Chins who stood staring upward, struck dumb at the sight of a man descending out of the moonlight. I landed fair atop one of them and knocked him flat, and the other two bolted into the hills shouting. I drank my fill at the river and walked the eight miles to camp. A nervous sentinel fired at me in the dark, but missed.\"\n\nThe Ethnologist began to laugh despite himself.\n\n\"When the relief column reached the ledge next morning, two more of the Sepoys had jumped before help arrived,\" the lieutenant added quietly. \"The rest were all right, barring a certain thirst.\"\n\nHe helped himself to soda and whisky and meditated happily for a moment.\n\n\"Wings as long as a mule,\" he said. \"And black feathers, seen by moonlight over the crests. Well, well. Your folk-lore collectors may thank me. Those children of the mountains have a better legend now than any I could have left them.\"",
